In Ladybird, an extension’s own documents get processes that host
nothing else: A process that has hosted one extension’s documents hosts
only that extension’s documents and about:blank documents nothing else
can reach, as process_to_host() already does for a WebUI’s documents.

Chromium keeps each extension in processes of its own, the same way:
ChromeContentBrowserClientExtensionsPart::IsSuitableHost() refuses an
enabled extension’s security principal a process that ProcessMap doesn’t
list for that extension, and refuses a process ProcessMap lists for any
extension to a principal that isn’t one. Gecko instead puts every
moz-extension: document in the one extension process that all extensions
share (IsolationBehavior::Extension in dom/ipc/ProcessIsolation.cpp, for
RemoteType::Kind::Extension), so Ladybird’s rule, like Chromium’s, is
the stricter one. WebKit sends an extension’s privileged identifier only
to the process of a page whose configuration requires that extension’s
base URL (WebExtensionController::parameters()).

Those are claims from reading each engine’s source code; none of the
engines was run to check them.
